WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.200
Every cell carries almost the same genome, so which genes are active?

2
00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:10.800
What makes a keratinocyte, a skin cell, differ from a macrophage, an immune cell, is

3
00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:12.220
which genes it transcribes.

4
00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:16.660
Transcription copies a stretch of DNA into RNA.

5
00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:21.960
Transcription factors and chromatin hold each cell's pattern in place.

6
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.120
Single-cell RNA sequencing is one way to read that pattern.

7
00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:34.690
First, the tissue is separated into single cells, usually with enzymes.

8
00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:40.130
Each captured RNA is copied into DNA so it can be sequenced.

9
00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:45.870
Before cells are mixed, every copy from one cell gets the same cell barcode.

10
00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:50.950
That barcode is a short sequence tag marking its cell of origin.

11
00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:54.980
Next, the pooled DNA is sequenced together.

12
00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:59.320
Cell barcodes sort every read back to its starting cell.

13
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:05.260
Counts fill a count matrix: one row per gene, one column per sampled cell.

14
00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:11.540
Each entry counts reads, or molecules when each copy carries a unique molecular

15
00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:17.440
identifier. Sequencing RNA counts which genes each cell uses right now.

16
00:01:18.433 --> 00:01:23.833
A count matrix holds many genes across many cells, so software groups cells with

17
00:01:23.833 --> 00:01:25.373
similar expression profiles.

18
00:01:26.753 --> 00:01:27.933
Each group is a cluster.

19
00:01:29.293 --> 00:01:34.073
Comparing a cluster's genes with known profiles to propose an identity is

20
00:01:34.073 --> 00:01:37.333
annotation. Notice the word propose.

21
00:01:38.433 --> 00:01:39.893
Counts can still mislead.

22
00:01:41.067 --> 00:01:47.007
Suppose you want the genes that nerve growth factor, NGF, controls in sympathetic

23
00:01:47.007 --> 00:01:48.607
neurons of living animals.

24
00:01:49.867 --> 00:01:52.047
Those neurons sit in groups called ganglia.

25
00:01:53.327 --> 00:01:56.367
Here is the catch: without NGF, the neurons die.

26
00:01:57.627 --> 00:02:03.247
If you compare RNA from normal ganglia and ganglia lacking NGF, what misleads your

27
00:02:03.247 --> 00:02:04.247
counts?

28
00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:12.810
Without living neurons, every neuron gene drops together.

29
00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:17.810
Your counts measure cell loss, not which genes NGF controls.

30
00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:21.170
So both groups must keep living neurons.

31
00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:23.790
A second limit is time.

32
00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:28.230
An RNA profile is a snapshot of active genes.

33
00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:33.950
A hidden fate bias, a leaning toward one future cell type, can escape it.

34
00:02:34.933 --> 00:02:37.273
That control worked in living animals.

35
00:02:38.693 --> 00:02:43.613
Deppmann, Ginty and colleagues used mice lacking Bax, a protein these neurons need

36
00:02:43.613 --> 00:02:48.753
to die. Without Bax, sympathetic neurons survive without NGF.

37
00:02:50.073 --> 00:02:54.413
They compared those ganglia with ganglia lacking both Bax and NGF.

38
00:02:55.613 --> 00:03:01.873
That gave NGF-dependent genes: transcription factors, synaptic genes, metabolism,

39
00:03:02.353 --> 00:03:03.933
cell size, and axon growth.

40
00:03:05.067 --> 00:03:07.107
A snapshot can also miss things.

41
00:03:08.547 --> 00:03:13.447
Weinreb, Klein, Camargo and colleagues gave mouse blood progenitors, the parent

42
00:03:13.447 --> 00:03:17.607
cells of blood, inherited DNA barcodes expressed as RNA.

43
00:03:18.907 --> 00:03:22.627
They sequenced some cells early and followed their sisters' progeny.

44
00:03:24.027 --> 00:03:28.767
Sister cells shared fate biases that the RNA profiles had not detected.

45
00:03:29.627 --> 00:03:33.767
RNA counts capture active genes now, not a cell's whole story.
